-- title:
--   Residue field of the local Hecke algebra comes from 𝒪
-- statement:
--   Fix a level $N \ge 1$ (given as a natural number with `NeZero N`) and a set $S$ of natural numbers, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation ring, i.e. a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring and is adically complete for its maximal ideal. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N$ with $q \notin S$, to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$. Assume, as a `Fact`, that [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N 2`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6) holds: the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule spanned by the weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are integers is the whole space. Write $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ for [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131), the localisation of `heckeBaseAlgebra N S 𝒪` at the multiplicative submonoid obtained by pushing the prime complement of the ideal `heckeCharKernel N S 𝒪 θ` forward along `latticeBaseChange N S 𝒪`. The conclusion is that the composite of the structure map $\mathcal{O} \to \mathbb{T}_\theta$ with the residue map of $\mathbb{T}_\theta$ onto its residue field is surjective.
--
--   This is the residue-field requirement imposed on a coefficient ring in deformation-theoretic arguments: the residue field of the local Hecke algebra attached to $\theta$ is generated by nothing beyond the image of the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$, although no identification of the two fields is asserted. It feeds the construction of a Hecke coefficient ring and, through that, the statements producing Galois representations that are flat or ordinary at prescribed primes.
